{"format":1,"seq":856,"writes":[{"type":"entry","value":{"model":[{"role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"I'm checking the title logic against the contract—if code is empty or whitespace-only, the title falls back to an empty string, which would trigger a \"no title\" warning from pi-action, a minor edge case. I'm also examining the result handling, particularly how the details field is cast when there's no error, wondering if it could be undefined in cases not covered by harness-written errors.\n\nIf details is undefined, checking a key on it would throw a TypeError, causing an unguarded fallback — I should add an `isRecord` guard there, and similarly for the saveOnly case where the shape of details.value might not match expectations. I'm also considering whether parsing code for dependencies could throw on syntax errors, whether truncation markers stay visible in the last 30 lines shown by pi-action, and whether missing code in call arguments is only handled safely within a catch block.\n\nThen I trace through how pi-action structures the display: it clones the full ToolResultMessage with content, details, and isError intact, preserves the original call arguments as sent by the model, and formats error summaries, titles, and call/result bodies consistently. I also note the type signature for ToolPresentation.call matches, and flag a process concern — this was pushed straight to main and released without going through a PR review.\n\nNot my main worry, but worth noting. I should check whether CI actually passed on main for that commit, since the run might still be in progress due to the release step. I'm also considering a stronger validation: installing pit v0.36.4 into pi-action and running Activity against a real tool presentation to see how it behaves end-to-end, though that setup might be heavy — I'll check CI status first before deciding if there's time for that.\n\n","thinkingSignature":""},{"type":"text","text":"The change is small. Be